      "targets": "product_rule_as_product_of_derivatives",
      "text": "To differentiate y = (x^2 + 1)(x^6 + 2), we use the product rule: (f*g)' = f'g + fg'.\nLet f = x^2 + 1 and g = x^6 + 2.\nf' = 2x\ng' = 6x^5\nSubstitute these into the formula:\n(x^2 + 1)'(x^6 + 2) + (x^2 + 1)(x^6 + 2)'\n2x(x^6 + 2) + (x^2 + 1)(6x^5)",
      "version": 236
    },
    {
      "item_id": "ca_prod_p1",
      "concept_id": "product_rule",
      "move": "remediate",
      "level": "worked_step",
      "targets": "product_rule_as_product_of_derivatives",
      "text": "To apply the product rule correctly, identify your two functions: f = (x^2 + 1) and g = (x^6 + 2).\n\nCalculate each derivative individually:\nf' = 2x\ng' = 6x^5\n\nNow substitute these into the formula f'g + fg':\n(2x)(x^6 + 2) + (x^2 + 1)(6x^5)",
      "version": 239
